  "worked_solution": "We wish to find the limits of integration ${u}_{1}(y,z)$ and ${u}_{2}(y,z)$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{(3-x)/3}{\\int }_{0}^{3-x-3y}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x={\u222c}_{D}[{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(y,z)}^{{u}_{2}(y,z)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the region of integration $R$ can be written as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{3-x}{3},\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 3-x-3y}\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x$ to $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}A,$ we write $R$ as a type II region. \n\nTo visualize this, imagine firing an arrow through the region parallel to the $x$-axis and in the direction of increasing $x,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/d26a7cc2c643b300226136a223be0fdebed4e17c99c2e562d1cc209caa023ea5.png)\n\nThe arrow enters the region through the back boundary and leaves through the front boundary:\n\n- The front boundary of the region, written in the form $x=x(y,z),$ is\n\n$$\nz=3-x-3y\\,\u27f9\\,x=3-3y-z.\n$$\n\n- The back boundary of the region is\n\n$$\nx=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the type II representation of $R$ is \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le x\\le 3-3y-z},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073682/assets/5641e6fc83054a355a2010084e1c8be2cd2d8144eae2aad0706704247f03b258.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration so that it is integrated first with respect to $x$ and then over $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{(3-x)/3}{\\int }_{0}^{3-x-3y}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\u222c}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{3-3y-z}f(x,y,z)\\,dx]dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
